# CiDispatchStalled, lifted VERBATIM out of prometheusrule.yaml — the rules file that
# queued-age.promtool-test loads. Not a manifest and not applied to anything: `promtool test
# rules` needs upstream-format rules (top-level `groups:`), and a PrometheusRule keeps them under
# `.spec`, so the CR cannot be loaded directly. Named .promtool-rules rather than .yaml on
# purpose — manifest-lint kubeconforms EVERY *.yaml under argocd/resources and a non-manifest
# there would fail it.
#
# THE COPY CANNOT DRIFT SILENTLY: `devbox run exporter-self-test` (gated in CI) reads the alert
# out of both files and asserts expr/for/labels are identical. homelab#288 (FU-158) owns the
# harness that will extract `.spec.groups` at run time and delete the copy altogether; this file
# is what makes the fixture runnable in the meantime.
#
# Regenerate after editing the alert in prometheusrule.yaml:
#   yq '{"groups": [{"name": "github", "rules": [.spec.groups[].rules[] | select(.alert == "CiDispatchStalled")]}]}' \
#     argocd/resources/github-exporter/prometheusrule.yaml
# …that command regenerates the `github` group ONLY (and carries the CR's comments across — drop
# them, the reasoning lives in the CR and this file is the runnable copy). The two witness groups
# at the bottom are not in the CR and must survive the re-copy — re-append them (see their own
# headers). The drift pin reads the block by alert NAME, so neither witness can shadow it.
groups:
  - name: github
    rules:
      - alert: CiDispatchStalled
        expr: max by (owner, runner) (time() - min_over_time(github_workflow_run_queued_since_timestamp{runner!="hosted"}[5m])) > 1800
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          platform_machinery: "true"
        annotations:
          summary: "CI cannot dispatch: {{ $labels.runner }} runs queued {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: 'The oldest {{ $labels.runner }} workflow run in {{ $labels.owner }} has waited {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} for a runner (normal pickup ~20s). A queued run never fails, so no CI-failure alert can see this. Which runs: github_workflow_run_queued_since_timestamp{runner!="hosted"}. Check in this order: (1) githubstatus.com / GithubVendorOutage — vendor-side, and do NOT re-run queued work; (2) saturation vs breakage — a saturated pool has jobs RUNNING, a broken one has workers WAITING (maxRunners is 4, argocd/platform/arc-runners.yaml); (3) kubectl -n arc-runners get pods,ephemeralrunnersets,autoscalinglisteners, then the LISTENER POD''s own log — the 2026-08-07 wedge (docs/incidents/2026-08-07-arc-listener-wedge.md) was visible ONLY there; (4) WAN reachability from each runner node — 2026-08-11 (docs/incidents/2026-08-11-wk-metal-02-default-route-loss.md) was one node''s missing default route with the listener healthy.'

  # ── REGRESSION WITNESS — NOT a deployed rule (the #310/#328 device, homelab#335) ───────────────
  # CiDispatchStalled exactly as it shipped from #284: the identity half (`max by (owner, runner)`,
  # which already drops pod/instance) and no hole half. It is the strongest form the alert ever had
  # while still being unable to hold `for: 10m` across a scrape gap — which is precisely the claim
  # #335 makes, so pairing the shipped alert against THIS keeps the contrast attributable to the
  # bridge alone. The hole case in queued-age.promtool-test checks both at the same eval times: the
  # shipped alert rides a 3-minute roll and pages on schedule at 41m, this one restarts its window
  # at the staleness marker and pages 8 minutes late.
  # A fossil, not a copy: if CiDispatchStalled's expr is revisited again, this stays as it is. It
  # is also why a STALE hand copy of the group above cannot pass vacuously — with the old expr in
  # both places the two alerts agree and every roll assertion in the fixture fails.
  - name: regression-witness-pre-335
    rules:
      - alert: CiDispatchStalledPre335
        expr: max by (owner, runner) (time() - github_workflow_run_queued_since_timestamp{runner!="hosted"}) > 1800
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "CI cannot dispatch: {{ $labels.runner }} runs queued {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"

  # ── COUNTER-WITNESS — the mechanical [10m] port, the one #335 REFUSED ──────────────────────────
  # Also not deployed, and not a fossil either: this is the rule that would have shipped if the
  # #331 class block had been applied to CiDispatchStalled by substitution. It exists so the
  # composition ruling in the CR is executed rather than asserted in prose — on the late-pickup
  # phantom case in queued-age.promtool-test it FIRES ~11 minutes after CI has recovered, on a run
  # that was never queued long enough to deserve a page, while the shipped [5m] expr stays silent
  # through the same series. `for: 10m` is deliberately identical: the range is the only variable.
  - name: witness-bridge-10m
    rules:
      - alert: CiDispatchStalledBridge10m
        expr: max by (owner, runner) (time() - min_over_time(github_workflow_run_queued_since_timestamp{runner!="hosted"}[10m])) > 1800
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "CI cannot dispatch: {{ $labels.runner }} runs queued {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
